ege it's  important that you never forget that we  certainly find ourselves in the midst of  a very tough stretch for personal family  reasons and for far broader ones I would  love to turn the clock back to New  Year's Day and give 2020 a second chance  or for that matter as many chances as it  might take to produce something closer  to what our fondest hope  imagined for the past five months when  the year began yet amidst all the  disruptions disappointments and dangers  of the recent months one reality looms  large although we are in the midst of a  pandemic  although domestic peace has broken down  especially in the streets of our great  cities although forces bent on rewriting  or repudiating our history and tearing  down our livelihoods and system of  government are running rampant  although the promising economy of  February has become the depressed  economy of March and April and beyond  although some of you may have forfeited  or had to defer job opportunities amidst  the economic chaos  although weddings had to be postponed or  modified  although Hillsdale College had to  embrace Universal online instruction for  a distressingly long period of time and  although the class of 2020 has had to  make up in a single weekend for what  should have been closer to two full  months of celebratory activities and  you've done an admirable job of that I  might add the world has not really  changed I was drawn to this theme for  tonight's address by recollections from  the last great national shock that we  all lived through 9/11 2001 many  commentators on that occasion claimed  that the attacks in New York and  Washington had quote changed the world  but I remember thinking even at that  time that that wasn't true the world had  not changed when the twin towers and  those four planes came crashing down  rather what had changed was our  understanding of the world we were  forced perhaps some for the first time  to realize that our country was not  completely secure from murderous attacks  from abroad and  there was such a thing as evil in the  world we have been re reminded of those  facts continually in the current year if  it's not international terrorists but  rather tiny microbes trying to kill us  today we still confront lethal dangers  and if the evil stalking us appears to  be more homegrown in 2020 than the  imported violence of 2001 nobody ever  said that evil can't come in many guises  all of these hard realities and many  others to confirm what has been true  since the dawn of humankind the world is  fallin filled with danger fraught with  risk forever flawed it is in fact a  battleground between good and evil the  battle won't end until God's appointed  time and in accordance with his eternal  plan this world moreover is not all  there is nor in the scheme of eternity  is it our final destination the whole of  the judeo-christian tradition affirms  these things and it is not within human  capacity fundamentally to change them  those who claim to have a prescription  for making the world a perfect place by  the way are the most dangerous radicals  of yesterday today and tomorrow all  richly deserving a place on the ash heap  of history  [Applause]  well if the cosmic story can sound grim  and the world worldly reality is beset  with troubles both are far from hopeless  there is plenty of goodness truth and  beauty in the world and we know as  people of faith that God has willed all  three to triumph in the end during the  time that we are given here it is our  highest calling to bear witness to these  things and to fight for them with every  breath Martin Luther put it this way in  his powerful him a mighty fortress is  our God  and though this world with Devils filled  should threaten to undo us we shall not  fear for God has willed his truth to  triumph through us if it's important for  the graduates not to succumb to  unrealistic hopes as you begin the next  chapters of your life it is equally  important for you to gird yourselves for  what you can and must contribute to the  right side of the battle that continues  to define the nature of the world and  our purpose in it in this commencement  season it is well to remember that yours  is certainly not the first class at  Hillsdale college to graduate into an  unusually turbulent and threatening  world imagine what any of the graduating  classes during the early 1860s must have  thought as they observed the realities  of the Civil War and weighed how they  should steer their lives through that  great national crisis for the bulk of  them the most immediate question was how  they could be useful in the fight to  abolish slavery  imagine what the graduating classes of  1917 and 1918 and 1941 through 1945 must  have thought as they braced themselves  for whatever part they would play in the  two great world wars where human freedom  and right and decency also hung in the  balance imagine what the graduates of a  succession of classes during the 1930s  must have thought as the worst economic  downturn in the nation's history  challenged and restricted their options  it is common knowledge around here how  Hillsdale graduates during the most  famous of our national crises rose to  the challenges of their times and it's  no accident that they did you must be no  different our hope and confidence are  that you will be no different six of you  have already chosen and qualified for  services officers in the United States  Marine Corps  many of you will pursue other kinds of  military and public service further  educational opportunities teaching  careers jobs and business and government  or full-time roles in the home as we sit  here I suspect that some of you are  unsure and perhaps anxious about what  you will be doing after this milestone  moment in your lives well take it from  someone who looks back with sheer wonder  at how the nearly 50 years since his own  college graduation actually turned out  you can't script a life it would take me  too long to recount all of the  unforeseen and unforeseeable twists and  turns in my own career all the lucky  breaks all the help I receive for  mentors colleagues and friends all the  kicks in the teeth along the way none of  us doubts that each of you will make  your own positive mark on the world God  wants you to and the Republic needs you  to the simplest advice I can give you is  always to be at your best learn what you  can from every experience good and bad  keep from doing and saying foolish or  idiotic things on social media devote  yourselves to the right things pray  without ceasing be patient and never  give up the fifteenth day of next month  will mark the 75th anniversary at the  end of World War two Japan the last of  our enemies still standing surrendered  on August 15 1945 VJ Day the end of  history's most murder  war stands as the greatest triumph of  what has been dubbed the greatest  generation of our own national story  it's important to make clear in any case  that we don't remember so fondly and  proudly the men and women of the World  War 2 era as the greatest generation  because they rid the world of evil  because they didn't and they couldn't we  give them special honor because they did  destroy the evils that most directly and  urgently threatened their their most  valuable principles and the nation that  they loved they did what their country  required of them without complaining and  without flinching  they won the battles that were before  them with God's help just as President  Roosevelt valved they would do once they  had vindicated the national cause and  helped to restore tens of millions of  people all over the world to a life of  freedom and self-government they played  the role of citizen with the same  selfless dedication and humility as they  had played the role of soldier this is  why we celebrate them and our made  poorer as they pass into eternity god  forbid that we should ever forget them  you need to be like them I submit to you  that our country thirsts for a new  greatest generation right now  [Applause]  history has shown before the threats we  face from abroad and from within and  from a virus that knows no borders  today's equivalent of the Manhattan  Project maybe operation warp speed the  race to come up with a vaccine for the  pandemic familiar suspects continue to  threaten us from abroad as they always  have and always will the 21st century  vandals and Huns imperiling us from  within actually speak English in many  cases college-educated English sometimes  Ivy League English but the stakes could  hardly be higher if they screamed at us  in a foreign tongue the measure of  greatness of any generation of our  countrymen has always been how well they  stood up to the threats against their  freedom and the security of their way of  life what's distinctive about today's  dangers the ones you will have to take  the lead in overcoming is how insidious  they are recent polls and recent actions  are extremely troubling within your  generation a recent poll claimed 18 to  35 year olds that is only a minority 35  to 40 percent assigned importance to  such vital elements as religion or  patriotism within the 55 and older age  group two-thirds claimed that religion  was important and 80% claimed that  patriotism was important in the midst of  the urban disturbances since the end of  May mostly useful youthful crowds have  burned and desecrated churches and the  flag  to face some of our most revered  national monuments and chanted that  America has never been great Mount  Rushmore the national anthem and the  very idea of Independence Day have been  disparaged in the media a democratic  congresswoman insists that the entire  economic and political system of the  United States needs to be torn down in  order to allow for true fairness and  equality  what is so alarming about this situation  is the compelling ignorance absence of  sound discernment and lack of historical  perspective on the part of those who  claim to show the nation the path to  positive change all manifesting  themselves against the backdrop of such  a precipitous decline in religious  belief and love of country as the rising  generation of active citizens and  leaders your ability to resist this  onslaught of darkness and to preserve  the Republic that is our common heritage  is going to be tested the quality of  your response will depend in large part  on what you know the value you attach to  what you know and the strength of your  own character permit me as one who is  quite aware of what you know to  highlight some of the most important  elements of this storehouse of truth  that must guide and sustain you through  what will surely be critically  challenging times ahead you know that  every human life whatever the race or  ethnicity age or gender born or unborn  is precious and deserves to be defended  none should ever be privileged or  advocated over others we are all made  equally in the image of God and that's  that if all lives matter you know that  livelihoods matter as well you know that  each of us bears a duty to ourselves to  family and to society to be as  enterprising as possible with our  god-given abilities and with the skills  and talents we have developed with the  help of others you know that human  beings are by nature social creatures  and meant to live in community  as the ancients and common sense alike  tell us the family is the foundation of  society and the body politic thus you  know that as families break down so will  society and politics you know  [Applause]  you know that order is the first need of  all these are Russell Kirk's words  authority whether public or parental  that cannot or will not keep order  within our various levels of community  is not worthy of the name you also know  that the foundation of politics in a  republic is self-government and that  self-government begins with self and  that when individuals have Mitchell II  yield to irrational disorderly or  destructive impulses community is  impossible you know that just as it is  in the nature of human beings to sin  nations will do likewise but you also  know that the history of our country has  been an ongoing quest in keeping with  our founding ideals to correct and atone  for our national sins and make the  imperfections of our Union something  closer to perfect on this day when a  genuine hero of the civil rights  movement John Lewis of Georgia has died  it behooves us all to rededicate  ourselves to the continuing effort to  achieve true racial harmony  you know in all humility that the more  you know doesn't necessarily make you  wiser or even smarter certainly the  endless flood of data careening down the  information Highway seems more to have  multiplied stupidity than reduced it you  know the need for good faith discourse  in the ongoing competition of ideas  rioting cancellation and the dictation  of angry mobs will never be suitable  substitutes for the honest and reasoned  debate that is supposed to be the glory  of democratic self-government you know  further that more and more distant and  remote ways of communicating with one  another and transacting our affairs is  not a prescription for a healthier  stronger society most importantly you  know that each one of us is beloved of  the God who created us and that this God  desires each one of us to pursue  fellowship with him to serve him as our  first love and allegiance to trust him  as our ever-present help in need and to  acknowledge him as the unshakable  foundation of our being as we  contemplate the parts we each will play  in trying to set right as much as we can  in this world more words from Luther's  already cited him remind us that we can  accomplish no truly good thing without  God's help and these words also offer  sure hope for how the great drama we are  all living will end here's the first  did we in our own strength confide our  striving would be losing we're not the  right man on our side the man of God's  own choosing  thus ask who that may be  Christ Jesus it is he Lord Sabbath his  name from age to age the same and he  must win the battle if any of this per  seat proceeding litany has seemed  self-evident or so commonly understood  especially within this circle as not to  merit restating permit me to share with  you a thought recently expressed in a  source sadly that I cannot recall but  which went something like this in times  of trial it is the responsibility of  every educated person to belabor the  obvious this reminds us of the advice  coaches typically give to their teams  when the teams are struggling or when  their coming up on a particularly big  game we've got to concentrate on the  fundamentals we've got to get the  fundamentals right indeed we do  perhaps as never before so in closing I  challenge the graduates and all of us  here to bring as much as we possibly can  of humanity society decency and  dutifulness to past present and future  into our households our places of work  and all levels of our communities affirm  the fundamentals seek the good be guided  by the truth what God has revealed to us  in his word and in the time-honored  wisdom of the ages love the beautiful  these have been the foundational  elements of your  education here much of the world is at  odds with them your strengths will  rejoice in the challenge of defending  and promoting them may God bless us  everyone to the task he's given us to do  may he always guide protect and prosper  the United States and this honorable  College thank you  [Applause]
 

